Unet architecture after Icenet
https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-25257-4

In this development:
Use NSIDC CDRv6 monthly grids, without 2d meteorological fields but with CPC teleconnection indices (telecon.indices) or CPC SOI indices (sstoi.indices)


Initial gemini framing of unet: gemini-code-1781279914576.py

unet4.py -- last month's sea ice grid only predictor

unet_multilayer.py -- last month's grid, sin, cos(month), sin,cos(2*month)
 n.b. Icenet used only sin and cos. Decomposing geophysical time series often shows significant contribution from the 2nd harmonic as well, so is tried. It turns out (unet_usage.py) that it does help the forecast here, too.

uneta.py -- add 2 months ago ice grid to predictors

unet_indices.py -- add AAO, AO, NAO, PNA (telecon.indices) to predictors

unet_usage.py -- evaluate the contribution each predictor (ice-1, ice-2, sin,cos(month), sin,cos(2*month), AAO, AO, NAO, PNA makes by permutation of each field in turn (randomizing the order of the grids for one variable at a time and seeing how much worse the MSE becomes).  Showed that the telecon.indices were not helpful, but 2nd harmonic is.  Temporarily tried a time variable (days since start of data / 3652.5). Not helpful here.

relag_trim.py -- fix reference to which field is lag1 and which is lag2. Trimmed out unhelpful variables.

looping -- main is to run a loop for a number of periods (20), each of with a number (5) of epochs, and then: save model at that point, produce sample forecast, and evaluate permutation relevance.
  -- also drop lag2 sea ice. Just using lag1 sea ice and the first and second harmonics of the seasonal cycle.
  n.b.: permutation relevance change substantially in early periods.

fcst.py -- make 12 forecasts of 1 month length from a given starting field
  n.b.: about 50 seconds per month
        survives whole year to at least casual eyeball level
        does not respect the land mask (that it isn't told about), putting sea ice over some land points, some of the time.





 


